INT. ROSELLE'S WEDDING CAKE ATELIER - MORNING

A pink-walled bakery in a state of panic. Racks of sugar flowers, bowls of fondant, a six-tier cake on a marble counter. A wall clock reads 8:47. The ceremony begins at ten.

BARTHOLOMEW "BART" OYELARAN, 30s, the groom, in a tuxedo shirt and no jacket, paces with a phone pinched between his chin and his shoulder. He has icing on his cuff.

CHLOE FENWICK-DASH, 30s, the bride, in a hoodie over a half-pinned gown, stands rigid before the cake. Her veil is askew. She stares at the third tier.

LUDWIK, 50s, the pastry chef, a mountain of apron and moustache, wrings a dish towel and says nothing.

DONNA (V.O.)
(from the phone, shrill)
Bartholomew! Is that a sunflower on my daughter's cake?

BART
(into the phone)
Mrs. Fenwick-Dash, it's a daisy.

DONNA (V.O.)
It is a sunflower, Bartholomew. I specifically said no sunflowers.

CHLOE
Mom, it's not a sunflower!

DONNA (V.O.)
Chloe, is that you? Why are you at the bakery? You're supposed to be at the salon!

CHLOE
The salon burned down.

BART
(whispering)
It did not burn down.

CHLOE
(whispering)
It's a metaphor.

LUDWIK
Madame, it is a daisy. Look. Five petals.

DONNA (V.O.)
I count nine!

Bart holds the phone out. Counts the petals. Brings it back.

BART
Seven.

DONNA (V.O.)
That is not a daisy, Bartholomew. That is a sunflower in a daisy costume.

Ludwik snatches a pastry knife. Slices the offending flower off the tier. It thumps to the counter. He hands the cake an apologetic pat.

LUDWIK
Now it is nothing.

DONNA (V.O.)
Better!

Chloe throws her bouquet at the wall. Bart ends the call. Silence, then, softly, from the counter:

CHLOE
Bart.

BART
Yeah?

CHLOE
Let's elope.

Ludwik hands them each a slice of the sunflower.
